    "created_at": "2025-12-16T14:14:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-16T14:14:33Z",
    "body": "Note that leaving the ongoing event dangling creates a weird behaviour with BFCache (currently shipped behaviour in chromium).\n\nGiven the following scenario:\n1. add listeners to `navigateerror` and `navigateEvent.signal.onabort`\n2. navigate away (cross-document)\n3. Go back (BFCache restore)\n4. Wait a bit if you want\n5. Perform a fragment navigation, `pushState`, or `window.stop()`\n\nThe `navigateerror` and `abort` event for the initial navigation would only be fired after (5)!\nThat's because, if the `ongoing navigate event` is not cleared as a result of a successful cross-document navigation, and lingers there until something like a same-document navigation or `window.stop()` aborts it.\n\nTest case: https://gist.github.com/noamr/cd99e6e2f7c0ddc7ee517077561288f4\n\nSome existing WPTs like [this one](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/156bb7cbb8483f6ffd4b275a1ed0b628fd0582cc/navigation-api/ordering-and-transition/transition-cross-document.html#L25) actually verify this behavior, by not expecting `navigateerror` to be fired on unload (though they don't verify what happens after BFCache).\n\nNote that as per spec [here](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#updating-the-traversable:set-the-ongoing-navigation-3), the ongoing event should be aborted and cleared when a document is unloaded.\n\nI think a clean solution for this would be to nullify the `ongoing navigate event` right before unloading a document.\nThis would make its abort signal never fire, as well as its `navigateerror` and `navigatesuccess` events.\n\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
